For the ideal op-amp with negative feedback, \[V_{+}=V_{-}.\] <img src="img/20571832e572499c.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> The inverting terminal is connected to a voltage divider consisting of two equal resistors of \(100\,\text{k}\Omega\). Hence, \[V_{-}=\frac{V_{o}}{2}.\] Therefore, \[V_{+}=\frac{V_{o}}{2}.\] Applying KCL at the non-inverting terminal, \[\frac{V_{+}-1}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{V_{+}-V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+I_{L}=0.\] Substituting \(V_{+}=V_{o}/2\), \[\frac{\frac{V_{o}}{2}-1}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{\frac{V_{o}}{2}-V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+I_{L}=0.\] The \(V_{o}\) terms cancel, giving \[-\frac{1}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}+I_{L}=0.\] Hence, \[I_{L}=\frac{1}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
=100\,\mu\text{A}.\] \[\boxed{100}\]

For the positive half-cycle (\(V_{in}&gt;0.6\,\text{V}\)), diode \(D_{1}\) is ON and diode \(D_{2}\) is OFF. The circuit behaves as an inverting amplifier. <img src="img/94fdcd847b306dcc.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[V_{o}
=
-\frac{R_f}{R_{in}}V_{in}
=
-\frac{16\,\text{k}\Omega}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}V_{in}
=
-1.6V_{in}.\] Since \[V_{in}=1\sin(3000t)\,\text{V},\] the negative peak output is \[V_{o,\text{peak}}
=
-1.6\times1
=
-1.6\,\text{V}.\] For the negative half-cycle (\(V_{in}&lt;-0.6\,\text{V}\)), diode \(D_{1}\) is OFF and diode \(D_{2}\) is ON. The output is disconnected from the op-amp, hence <img src="img/386c86cb84a2bc56.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[V_{o}=0.\] Therefore, the magnitude of the negative peak output is \[|V_{o,\text{peak}}|
=
1.6\,\text{V}.\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The circuit is a precision half-wave rectifier.</li><li>During the negative half-cycle, \(D_{1}\) conducts while \(D_{2}\) is OFF.</li></ul></div>

For an ideal op-amp with negative feedback, \[V_{+}=V_{-}=0\,\text{V}.\] Let the voltage at the divider node be \(V_{1}\). <img src="img/22aec96ab654f5f2.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Applying KCL at node \(V_{1}\), \[\frac{V_{1}-2}{20\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{V_{1}}{20\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{V_{1}-0}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Multiplying throughout by \(20\,\text{k}\Omega\), \[(V_{1}-2)+V_{1}+2V_{1}=0\] \[4V_{1}=2\] \[V_{1}=0.5\,\text{V}.\] Applying KCL at the inverting terminal, \[\frac{V_{1}-0}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
=
\frac{0-V_{o}}{20\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Hence, \[V_{o}
=
-\frac{20\,\text{k}\Omega}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}V_{1}
=
-2(0.5)
=
-1\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{-1}\]

Let total work \(= 1\) unit. Establish individual rates: \[\text{Rate}_Q = \frac{1}{25}\,\text{/day},\qquad
    \text{Rate}_R = \frac{1}{50}\,\text{/day}\] <strong>Q's effective work in 7 days:</strong><br/>Q works 12,h/day normally, but took sick leave for the first 2 days \(\Rightarrow\) Q worked for \(7 - 2 = 5\) days: \[W_Q = 5 \times \frac{1}{25} = \frac{5}{25} = \frac{1}{5}\] <strong>R's effective work in 7 days:</strong><br/>R works 12,h/day normally but worked 18,h/day for all 7 days. Ratio of hours: \(\dfrac{18}{12} = \dfrac{3}{2}\): \[W_R = 7 \times \frac{1}{50} \times \frac{3}{2}
        = \frac{7 \times 3}{100}
        = \frac{21}{100}\] <strong>Ratio</strong> \(W_Q : W_R\): \[\frac{1}{5} : \frac{21}{100}
    = \frac{20}{100} : \frac{21}{100}
    = 20 : 21\] \[\boxed{\text{C)}\ 20{:}21}\]

Given: \((9\,\text{inches})^{\frac{1}{2}}
         = (0.25\,\text{yards})^{\frac{1}{2}}\). Verify: \(1\,\text{yard} = 36\,\text{inches}\), so \(0.25\,\text{yards} = 9\,\text{inches}\). \(\checkmark\) Use this to find the conversion: squaring both sides of \((9)^{1/2} = (0.25)^{1/2}\) in their respective units gives the consistent ratio. Check option D: \(81\,\text{inches} = 0.0625\,\text{yards}\)? \[81\,\text{inches} = \frac{81}{36}\,\text{yards}
                      = 2.25\,\text{yards} \neq 0.0625\] Check option B: \(9\,\text{inches} = 1.5\,\text{yards}\)? \[9\,\text{inches} = \frac{9}{36}\,\text{yards}
                     = 0.25\,\text{yards} \neq 1.5\] Check option C: \(9\,\text{inches} = 0.25\,\text{yards}\)? \[\frac{9}{36} = 0.25 \quad \checkmark \quad \text{TRUE}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) 9 inches = 0.25 yards}}\]

A clock seen in a mirror shows 1:30. To find the actual time, subtract the mirror time from 11:60 (or 12:00 for hour-hand symmetry): \[\text{Actual time} = 11{:}60 - 1{:}30 = 10{:}30\] Wait — the problem states ``two and a quarter hours back, the reflection showed 1:30''. So the mirror showed 1:30 two and a quarter hours ago: \[\text{Actual time when mirror showed 1:30}
    = 12{:}00 - 1{:}30 = 10{:}30\] \[\text{Current actual time}
    = 10{:}30 + 2{:}15 = 12{:}45\] \[\boxed{\text{D) 12:45}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Mirror time formula: Actual \(= 11{:}60 -\) mirror reading (or \(12{:}00\) if minutes \(= 00\)).</li><li>Add the elapsed time to the actual time at the moment of the mirror reading.</li></ul></div>

Set up a coordinate system with the start point at the origin. <strong>Path of \(M\):</strong><br/>\[\text{10\,km East} \to (10,\,0);
    \quad\text{then 10\,km North-East (45°)}\] \[\Delta x = 10\cos45^{\circ} = \frac{10}{\sqrt{2}},\quad
    \Delta y = 10\sin45^{\circ} = \frac{10}{\sqrt{2}}\] \[M_{\text{final}} = \left(10 + \frac{10}{\sqrt{2}},\;
                              \frac{10}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
                     \approx (17.07,\;7.07)\] <strong>Path of \(N\):</strong><br/>\[\text{5\,km South} \to (0,\,-5);
    \quad\text{then 4\,km South-East (SE = 45° below East)}\] \[\Delta x = 4\cos(-45^{\circ}) = \frac{4}{\sqrt{2}},\quad
    \Delta y = 4\sin(-45^{\circ}) = -\frac{4}{\sqrt{2}}\] \[N_{\text{final}} = \left(\frac{4}{\sqrt{2}},\;
                            -5 - \frac{4}{\sqrt{2}}\right)
                     \approx (2.83,\;-7.83)\] <strong>Distance \(MN\):</strong><br/>\[d = \sqrt{(17.07-2.83)^{2} + (7.07+7.83)^{2}}
      = \sqrt{(14.24)^{2} + (14.90)^{2}}
      = \sqrt{202.8 + 222.0}
      = \sqrt{424.8}
      \approx 20.61\,\text{km}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) }20.61\,\text{km}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Decompose each leg into \((x, y)\) components using trigonometry; accumulate to find final positions.</li><li>Distance \(= \sqrt{(\Delta x)^{2} + (\Delta y)^{2}}\).</li></ul></div>

Revenue \(=\) units sold \(\times\) price per unit. Prices: Elegance = Rs.,48, Smooth = Rs.,63, Soft = Rs.,78, Executive = Rs.,173. Total annual revenue per product: \[R_{\text{Elegance}}
      = 48(27300+25222+28976+21012) = 48 \times 102510
      = 4{,}920{,}480\] \[R_{\text{Smooth}}
      = 63(20006+19392+22429+18229) = 63 \times 80056
      = 5{,}043{,}528\] \[R_{\text{Soft}}
      = 78(17602+18445+19544+16595) = 78 \times 72186
      = 5{,}630{,}508\] \[R_{\text{Executive}}
      = 173(99999+9942+10234+10109) = 173 \times 130284
      = 22{,}539{,}132\] Executive contributes the greatest fraction of revenue. \[\boxed{\text{B) Executive}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Revenue \(=\) price \(\times\) total units sold over all four quarters.</li><li>Despite lower volume, Executive's high price (Rs.,173) dominates total revenue.</li></ul></div>

Cycles to failure decreases exponentially with load: \(N = A \cdot e^{-kL}\). Given: \[L = 80 \Rightarrow N = 100, \qquad
    L = 40 \Rightarrow N = 10{,}000\] From the two data points: \[\frac{N_1}{N_2} = \frac{100}{10{,}000} = e^{-k(80-40)}
    \implies e^{-40k} = \frac{1}{100}
    \implies -40k = -\ln 100
    \implies k = \frac{\ln 100}{40}\] Find \(L\) when \(N = 5000\): \[\frac{5000}{10{,}000} = e^{-k(L-40)}
    \implies e^{-k(L-40)} = 0.5\] \[-k(L-40) = -\ln 2
    \implies L - 40 = \frac{\ln 2}{k}
    = \frac{\ln 2 \times 40}{\ln 100}
    = \frac{0.6931 \times 40}{4.6052}
    \approx 6.02\] \[L = 40 + 6.02 \approx 46.02\] The load for 5000 cycles failure \(\approx 46.02\). \[\boxed{\text{B) }46.02}\]

For a quadratic with roots \(\alpha, \beta\): product \(\alpha\beta = 4\). \[\frac{\alpha^{n} + \beta^{n}}{\alpha^{-n} + \beta^{-n}}
    = \frac{\alpha^{n} + \beta^{n}}
           {\dfrac{\alpha^{n} + \beta^{n}}{\alpha^{n}\beta^{n}}}
    = \alpha^{n}\beta^{n}
    = (\alpha\beta)^{n}
    = 4^{n}\] \[\boxed{\text{B) }4^{n}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(\alpha^{-n} + \beta^{-n}
            = \dfrac{\alpha^{n}+\beta^{n}}
                    {(\alpha\beta)^{n}}\).</li><li>The ratio simplifies directly to \((\alpha\beta)^{n}\).</li></ul></div>

Count rectangles in a \(2 \times 4\) grid of unit cells. A rectangle is defined by choosing 2 of the 3 horizontal lines and 2 of the 5 vertical lines: \[\binom{3}{2} \times \binom{5}{2}
    = 3 \times 10 = 30\] \[\boxed{\text{C)}30}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For an \(m \times n\) grid of unit cells, the number of rectangles \(= \dbinom{m+1}{2} \times \dbinom{n+1}{2}\).</li><li>Here \(m=2\), \(n=4\): \(\dbinom{3}{2}\times\dbinom{5}{2} = 3\times10
            = 30\).</li></ul></div>

\[(x\%\ \text{of}\ y) + (y\%\ \text{of}\ x)
    = \frac{xy}{100} + \frac{yx}{100}
    = \frac{2xy}{100}\] This equals \(2\%\) of \(xy\), or equivalently: \[= \frac{2xy}{100} = 2\%\ \text{of}\ xy\] Checking option B: \(2\%\) of \((xy/100)\) \(= \dfrac{2}{100} \cdot \dfrac{xy}{100}
  = \dfrac{xy}{5000}\) — this is different. Re-check: \(\dfrac{2xy}{100} = 2\%\) of \(xy\). \(\checkmark\) \[\boxed{\text{A) }2\%\ \text{of}\ xy}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>\(x\%\) of \(y = \dfrac{xy}{100}\); adding the symmetric term gives \(\dfrac{2xy}{100} = 2\%\) of \(xy\).</li></ul></div>

A square pyramid: base perimeter \(= x\), so base side \(= x/4\). Slant height \(= x/2\) (half the perimeter). Lateral surface area \(=\) perimeter of base \(\times\) slant height \(\div 2\): \[LSA = \frac{1}{2} \times x \times \frac{x}{2}
        = \frac{x^{2}}{4}
        = 0.25\,x^{2}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) }0.25\,x^{2}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Lateral surface area of pyramid \(= \dfrac{1}{2} \times \text{perimeter}
            \times \text{slant height}\).</li></ul></div>
